yeah, definitely I would
say expand your community.

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:11.247
One of the things that I had growing up,

00:21:11.247 --> 00:21:12.690
and you may be familiar
with this, you know,

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:14.250
just a lot of mentors.

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:16.440
And I had, I used to say, I
would joke and I would say,

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:19.200
I had multiple mamas, so
different, you know, these,

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:21.000
some of your mom's friends
or there were women

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.280
who just where like to sort of take you on

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:25.290
as one of their son or
something; they were supportive.

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:29.590
Or mentors, you know, who
became part of that community

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:33.150
and provided some of that
support and that affirmation,

00:21:33.150 --> 00:21:34.800
that accountability, and
all the things I needed.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.850
So, my comment to those folks would be

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:39.900
to sort of expand your
community a little bit.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:41.730
Go beyond sort of the confounds

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:45.570
of what your community looks like now.

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:49.080
And find those people,
you know, who can be

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:50.760
sort of the supportive folks.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:51.780
And they're out there.

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:52.717
They are out there.

00:21:52.717 --> 00:21:54.300
There're plenty of, obviously
there are organizations

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:57.796
out there that have mentors

00:21:57.796 --> 00:22:02.220
and provide mentoring
services and other things.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:04.203
But whether it's a church, a club,

00:22:05.550 --> 00:22:09.000
you know, YMCA, you know,
different affinity groups,

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.220
a hobby, you know, there's
so many different places now

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:14.160
to find supportive community, I think.

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:16.053
But, I don't know.

00:22:17.111 --> 00:22:17.944
- [Nii] That's good.

00:22:17.944 --> 00:22:19.211
- I don't know if that makes sense to you

00:22:19.211 --> 00:22:21.428
as in your profession, Nii.

00:22:21.428 --> 00:22:22.440
- Yeah, I mean I think that's really,

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:23.730
those are really wise words, too,

00:22:23.730 --> 00:22:25.890
especially as you defined it earlier on

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:27.630
that if that's not happening
within the community

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:29.910
people have already to
be able to and be willing

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:32.430
to look elsewhere, and to
be able to get that support

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:34.770
of people who have that like mindset.

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:36.660
So I think that's really, really important

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:39.150
that you shared that and
really, really helpful advice.

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:40.980
I'm also curious 'cause I
know you've mentioned faith

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:43.530
and faith communities,
as well, a few times.

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:46.770
So how is that, what's that
aspect of your journey?

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:47.700
What has that looked like

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:49.410
and how's that continue to play a role?

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:50.613
- Yeah, wow.

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:57.770
I don't know if we could
have survived the civil war

00:22:57.770 --> 00:23:00.210
in Liberia, and we lived
through some pretty,

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:03.720
you know, pretty scary things
for a period of time there

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.930
in terms of just escaping firefights,

00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:08.130
being held at gunpoints,

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:11.790
gunpoint, excuse me, a few times,

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:14.763
dealing with starvation
and food shortages.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:16.830
I mean, there were a lot of scary things

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:21.830
but we always had our faith
in God and just in prayer.

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:26.700
And I've come to find
that the Bible's probably

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:28.440
one of the best, if not the best book

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:30.467
of mental health, actually, you know.

00:23:30.467 --> 00:23:31.587
And this is, I don't know too many people

00:23:31.587 --> 00:23:35.430
who would agree with that, but
as you sort of read the Bible

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:37.020
and really get to know the stories

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:40.366
and you think about the trauma that Daniel

00:23:40.366 --> 00:23:44.040
and Shadrach, Meshach, and
Abednego, or David, or Joseph.

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.415
I mean, people went through some really,

00:23:46.415 --> 00:23:48.300
and of course, Jesus himself, of course,

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:49.830
you know, some really difficult things.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:51.930
And you see how those difficult things,

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.510
the ark of those things led to victory,

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:58.260
you know, (audio distortion)
or while they were still alive.

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:02.790
And I thought, and so those
things became sort of part

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:05.820
of how we lived our lives.

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:09.540
And yeah, it's a big part of
faith is, it's almost like

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:12.390
I can't see my life without my
faith in God, I really can't.

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:15.990
I mean, I'd be, you know, I
won't say because of my mom.

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:17.181
She probably doesn't like me to say that

00:24:17.181 --> 00:24:18.660
so I might not be the
best Christian, right,

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:20.640
if I have to be honest with myself.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:25.640
But I do believe and
whatever little faith I have,

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:28.860
I think continues to make
just this huge difference

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:32.760
in how I navigate the ups and downs,

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:35.550
and the (audio distortion)
of life, for sure.

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:36.780
- Yeah, that's really powerful.

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:39.690
I mean, I appreciate even the
way that you just framed that

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:42.360
even how you, you know,
read through the narratives

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:44.143
in the Bible and the mental
health ark that's there.

00:24:44.143 --> 00:24:47.490
I don't think we always
give as much acknowledgment

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:50.130
or credit to as we should
about how difficult

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:51.420
a lot of those circumstances were.

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:52.500
And so that's really-

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:54.660
- Yeah, they seem like
stories but they're real.

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:56.520
And I admit it too, you
probably define yourself

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.613
in those stories, you
realize, oh yeah, okay.

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.640
Yeah, you can sort of go
through the same process

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:05.250
and come on the other
side and be resilient

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:08.643
and be a victor, and so forth.

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:10.590
- Yeah, really powerful.

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:11.850
And so may aspects of community

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:14.370
within those stories, as
well, even as you were talking

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:15.300
about in your own experience.

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:16.620
I mean, there's so many parallels there

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:19.710
and things that we can
definitely learn along the way.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:21.360
And to push on that a little
bit, I know you talked

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:23.340
about your faith in God and just reading

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:26.010
through those stories, you
know, the practice of prayer.

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:28.140
How's the community of faith?

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:31.200
Has that also been part
of the journey, as well?

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:32.580
- Yeah, it's definitely yeah.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:36.200
And as I mentioned, in Liberia, we had...

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:39.300
When we were living through the war

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:42.060
and well prior to that,
we were part of a church

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:46.980
and, you know, used to host
a Bible study at our home.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:50.790
And so we had that community
and then (audio distortion)

00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:53.190
when we lived through
the war, we still had

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:54.183
that faith community, so there were people

00:25:54.183 --> 00:25:58.470
we could read the Bible
with and pray with,

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:00.510
and even just talk through
sort of the experiences

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:04.410
we were having and encourage
one another and that, okay,

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:05.243
"You know what?

00:26:06.236 --> 00:26:08.673
"God can see us through this

00:26:08.673 --> 00:26:10.290
"and we're just gonna
continue to trust Him."

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:13.230
Obviously, you know, some people made it

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:14.386
and some people didn't make it

00:26:14.386 --> 00:26:19.386
and, you know, why that
happened that way, I don't know.

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:23.250
But we certainly, our faith
community really was important

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:26.790
and it was a kind of a perfect,
and I'll call it therapy,

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:29.610
or whatever, but to help us navigate

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:30.900
what was a very difficult time.

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:33.810
And that's become really
true even through now.

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:36.480
And, you know, I've had
other challenges in my life

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:39.870
for this house or family,
and I've been able to,

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:41.340
that faith community's always been there

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:46.340
to kind of give me the
strength to sort of see my way

00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:49.860
through whatever the obstacles were.

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:51.780
- Yeah, again, just so powerful

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:53.370
and so brave at the same time.

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:55.560
I know, depending on when we're talking,

00:26:55.560 --> 00:26:57.600
where we're talking
about faith, either here

00:26:57.600 --> 00:26:59.070
in the US or different parts of the world,

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:01.470
it can look very different
in different situations.

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:05.580
And knowing that in some
situations, people are navigating

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:08.596
through a whole host of
challenges that sometimes some

00:27:08.596 --> 00:27:10.650
of us in the states can't even relate to.

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:13.410
People who have had to become refugees

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:15.150
or take asylum because
of all these things.

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:17.100
And to hear, always to hear stories

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:18.750
of people holding on to their faith

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:20.640
in the midst of those
challenging situations.

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:22.590
And even as you mentioned,
not everybody made it,

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:25.500
but how that was such a
bedrock and a foundation

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:26.880
I think is so powerful and something

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:29.160
that I hope we're paying
more and more attention to

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:30.810
in general, but then also just in terms

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:31.830
of how it affects our lives,

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:32.663
how it affects our mental health.

00:27:32.663 --> 00:27:36.540
And so, again, it's just
powerful, encouraging,

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:39.840
and sobering to hear you kind
of walk through those pieces

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:41.400
of your story, as well.

00:27:41.400 --> 00:27:42.450
- Yeah.

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:43.760
That's cool.

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:45.150
- So I'm gonna pivot a little bit

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:47.760
'cause I know, and as you know,
we pull on a lot of topics

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:50.370
on this podcast and we're
getting to that already.

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:53.220
We've already talked
about some of the culture

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:55.830
and mental health, and faith.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:58.350
But in terms of race,
that was another topic

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:00.390
that I wanted to kind of
talk through, as well.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:01.530
Especially with everything that's happened

00:28:01.530 --> 00:28:03.360
in the last few years,
but then also knowing

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:06.450
that conversations about
race are different,

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:08.850
different and overlapping in
different parts of the world.

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:10.860
So I'd be just curious if you'd share

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:14.250
with our listeners your
perspective on race

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:17.970
and race relations both
from the context of Liberia,

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:19.380
and for those of you listening,

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:21.450
Yu-Jay's smiling right now
so I'm sure he has a lot

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:23.340
to share both from the context

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:25.890
of Liberia and then the states.

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:28.380
And then, you know, there's
some comparisons we might jump

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:30.300
into later, even from my own experience.

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:31.980
But I'll leave it there for now.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:32.813
- Yeah, you do that.

00:28:32.813 --> 00:28:33.646
- Don't wanna open it up too much.

00:28:33.646 --> 00:28:35.070
(Nii chuckling)

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:37.133
- That's some pretty heavy
(audio distortion) lift.

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:39.730
I'm thinking am I supposed
to dunk this thing

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:42.210
or am I gonna barely get
(audio distortion), man.

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:47.210
But, yeah, and so I will say
that in Liberia, we had tribes

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:52.110
which, and I know I don't think a lot

00:28:52.110 --> 00:28:55.050
of people are familiar
with the idea of tribes,

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:58.347
but they're essentially just
subgroups within the country.

00:28:58.347 --> 00:29:00.150
And we have different dialects

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:02.280
and we called 'em dialects
or you know, languages.

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:05.130
And you may have to add a better context

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:07.170
to some of this stuff here, but
I'm just gonna kinda explain

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:09.720
the way that I know it.

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:13.380
And so, my family was from one tribe

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:17.733
but we were all obviously
dark skin, Black people.

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:21.540
But there was, you
notices, there were sort

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:23.610
of stereotypes and differences based

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:25.923
on what tribes you came from.

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:29.610
And so I had a tribal context.

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:32.850
But then coming to the US, now you've got

00:29:32.850 --> 00:29:36.570
a skin color racial
context, which is different.

00:29:36.570 --> 00:29:40.080
And I grew up when I came
here, I sort of grew up

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:42.133
in a very predominantly white world.

00:29:42.133 --> 00:29:44.493
And I have a friend of
mine who's jokingly said

00:29:44.493 --> 00:29:48.240
that I was white socialized,
like yeah, I guess.

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:50.973
Okay, but you know, so
going through prep school,

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.900
prep college, and other
schools that were, you know,

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:59.059
there were not a lot of
Black and Brown people

00:29:59.059 --> 00:30:00.403
and really enjoying those experiences.

00:30:00.403 --> 00:30:02.670
I actually loved those experiences

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:05.793
and the values they sort
of inculcated me with,

00:30:06.855 --> 00:30:08.970
and you know, so I have no regrets.

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:11.190
But it was not till college
that I really started

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:12.167
to understand myself as,

00:30:12.167 --> 00:30:14.880
that I started to explore
myself as a Black man in America

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:17.910
and what that really means.

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:18.867
That was a relatively
interesting experience.

00:30:18.867 --> 00:30:20.250
And I started taking, I took some

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:22.200
African American lit
classes and did readings

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.656
of Ralph Ellison, Derrick Bell,

00:30:23.656 --> 00:30:26.040
and you know, DuBois
and all these different,

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:30.630
and then some Purnell,
West, and Henry Louis Gates.

00:30:30.630 --> 00:30:31.800
I mean, like that's when I'd written...

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:36.000
That was, I was what, 19 years old?

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:39.210
And anyway when I started to kind of go

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:41.070
through this actually a self actualization

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:42.750
of oh, I'm a Black man in America.

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:44.760
That's different from being
a Black man in Africa.

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:46.050
I'm like Liberian.

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:49.470
And learning that and then
trying to sort of embrace that

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:54.450
and still be true to
the fact that I grew up

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:56.970
in what people might, I
mean, I call it American,

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:58.590
but people might call a white world,

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:00.510
and I was very comfortable in that space.

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:01.710
And I still am.

00:31:01.710 --> 00:31:04.110
And then there's this Liberian part of me

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:06.990
that I value and you know,
I don't wanna lose either.

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:08.910
And so then there's the
African American side of me

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:11.520
so I never, I had to kind of realize,

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:13.140
I'm a multi-cultural person.

00:31:13.140 --> 00:31:14.250
I never even-
- Yeah.

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:15.130
- Right?

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:16.860
Like, oh my goodness, I'm
a multi-cultural person.

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:19.920
So my feel on race is
it matters, you know,

00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:22.560
kind of going from Cornel
West's book "Race Matters".

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:24.990
I think it was one of the
first book I read on race.

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:29.990
And it's important and I
think there's some beautiful,

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:32.400
you know, sort of distinctions
that I think makes

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:34.440
us as a community better.

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:37.060
It is hard for me to sort of

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:44.150
to kind of understand the
conflicts that we still see here,

00:31:44.190 --> 00:31:45.630
you know, racially, in America.

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:48.990
It's something that I don't
know how much of it I,

00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:52.710
you know, it's more kind of the lenses

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:54.510
through which we see the
world and how much of it is

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:55.710
the reality of the world.

00:31:55.710 --> 00:31:56.903
Do you know what I mean?

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:01.560
There's a quote I saw not too long ago

00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:04.110
from I think from the Talmud
and it said we see the world,

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:06.783
we see things not as
they are, but as we are.

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:11.260
And this idea of okay, what
is it that I'm projecting

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:13.920
to sort of what happening around me?

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:15.270
And then what is it that's actually true

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:18.750
in terms of my actual experience of racism

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:21.900
or discrimination or any inequity.

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.223
So I don't know if I
answered your question

00:32:24.223 --> 00:32:25.170
but I thought my-
- Definitely.

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:26.043
- My perspective.

00:32:26.952 --> 00:32:30.047
It's something that I'm
growing to more and more.

00:32:30.047 --> 00:32:33.180
And to understanding and then hopefully,

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:34.920
navigating it in a healthy way.

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:35.753
- Yeah, that's so important.

